 whether the proposed bound is sharp, and if not, whether a conjecture for the sharp bound was formulated and subsequently proved by another researcher. We highlight the cases in which the bound was not sharp but a conjecture for the sharp bound was proposed, yet remained unproven. In this paper, we establish the proof of one such conjecture, while leaving our methodology available to researchers for addressing the other conjecture, which likewise remains an open problem.\n\nLupaş et al. [17] introduced the following class of functions, which is also presented in Table 1:\n\n{S}_{\\mathrm{Sym}}^{\\ast }:=\\left\\{f\\in \\mathcal{A}:{\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\xi {f}^{\\prime }\\left(\\xi \\right)}{f\\left(\\xi \\right)-f(-\\xi )}}={\\phi }_{H}\\left(\\omega \\left(\\xi \\right)\\right),\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{for}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{some}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\omega \\in \\mathbb{B},\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{and}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{every}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\xi \\in \\mathbb{U}\\right\\},\n\nwhere this class is characterized via the analytic function {\\phi }_{H}:\\mathbb{U}\\to \\mathbb{C} (see Figure 1), which satisfies the following formula\n\n{\\phi }_{H}\\left(\\xi \\right)=1+\\xi +{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}}{\\xi }^{2}-{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{9}}{\\xi }^{3},\\phantom{\\rule{1.em}{0ex}}\\xi \\in \\mathbb{U}.\n\n(3)\n\nSee also [20].\n\nThey presented several sharp inequalities, such as those for the initial coefficients, the Fekete–Szegö inequality, and the second-order Hankel determinant. However, they provided the best possible upper bound for the third-order Hankel determinant, namely 0.104485, which is not sharp. At the same time, they proposed the following conjecture, which is sharp:\n\nConjecture 1 \n\n([17], p. 12). If f\\in {\\mathcal{S}}_{\\mathrm{Sym}}^{\\ast }, then the sharpness of the third Hankel bound is given by\n\n|{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{16}}.\n\nThe earlier approach in Lupaş et al. [17] provided a significant estimate by applying coefficient inequalities and a parametrization of Carathéodory functions. In the present work, we refine this strategy in three respects:\n\n\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\hfill {\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)=& {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{331776}}(-184{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{6}-552{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{2}+2592{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{3}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{3}+2628{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{2}^{2}-6048{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{4}\\hfill \\\\ & +2592{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{2}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{3}-6048{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{2}^{3}+10368{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{2}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{4}-5184{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{3}^{2}).\\hfill \\end{array}\n\nUtilizing (5)–(8) and simplifying the resulting expressions, we obtain\n\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\hfill 552{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{2}& =276\\left({\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{6}+(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}\\alpha \\right),\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill 2592{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{3}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{3}& =648{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{6}+1296(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}\\alpha -648(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}{\\alpha }^{2}+1296{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{3}(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){(1-|\\alpha |}^{2})\\beta ,\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill 657{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{2}^{2}& =657{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{6}+1314(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}\\alpha +657{(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2})}^{2}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}{\\alpha }^{2},\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill 6048{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{4}& =756{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{6}+2268(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}\\alpha +3024(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}{\\alpha }^{2}-2268(4-{\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{2}){\\mathsf{\\ell }}_{1}^{4}{\\alpha }^{